    "encrypted": "false",
    "long_instructions": "## Make: Code Your Data Story\r\n\r\nYour Task: Write the code that finds the total and the average of your numbers. Use AI Tutor to draw your picture.\r\n\r\nDo This:\r\n\r\n1. Open `story_plan.txt`. Set `story` and `count` in `main.py` to match your plan.\r\n2. Write your code on the lines marked `YOUR CODE`: start `total` at 0, ask for each number with `input()` (as an `int`), add it to `total`, find the `average` after the loop, and print the total and the average.\r\n3. Ask AI Tutor to draw your picture, using MY AI PROMPT. Paste the code you keep into the `AI SLOT` markers. AI Tutor draws. You write the sum and the average.\r\n4. Predict what your picture will look like, then run your program.\r\n5. Use Validate to check your algorithm.\r\n\r\nChallenge:\r\n\r\nAfter Validate passes, store `total % count == 0` in a variable, print it, and show it in your picture.",
    "offer_browser_tts": "false",
    "submittable": "false",
    "disable_edit_run_for_submission": "false",
    "hide_share_and_remix": "false",
    "enable_micro_bit": "false",
    "mini_app": "theater",
    "widget_view": "false",
    "widget_view_allow_show_code": "false",
    "mini_app_variant": "functional",
    "predict_settings": {
      "isPredictLevel": false
    },
    "ai_tutor_prompt_settings": {
      "answerTypes": [
        "buildPython",
        "documentation",
        "example"
      ],
      "answerTypeCustomizations": {
        "buildPython": "Data Story levels: drawing code only.\n\nScope\n- Write drawing code only. Never write, change, or suggest code that reads input or calculates the total, the average, or splits_evenly. If a student asks for it, say it is their code, point to their YOUR CODE lines, and offer to explain the pattern instead.\n- Edit only inside the AI SLOT regions. Do not change settings (story, count), YOUR CODE lines, the for loop line, or the print lines. If something outside a slot should change, such as a title that does not match the plan, say so in your explanation instead of changing it.\n- Use the variables the student's plan names (value, j, average, and splits_evenly if mentioned). Never reassign them. Give any new variable its own name, such as bar_x. Define scale numbers (like the maximum value or the picture height) once, in Slot 1, and reuse them.\n- Fill every slot the student's picture needs: setup in Slot 1, one number in Slot 2, the average in Slot 3. If a slot stays empty, say which one and why.\n\nTheater rules (breaking any of these stops the program with an error)\n- Colors: use ONLY these names: white, silver, gray, black, red, maroon, yellow, olive, lime, green, aqua, teal, blue, navy, fuchsia, purple, pink, orange, gold, brown, chocolate, tan, turquoise, indigo, violet, beige, ivory. Or use a hex color with six digits, like \"#006400\", or Color(red, green, blue) with whole numbers from 0 to 255. Any other name stops the program. That includes darkgreen, crimson, skyblue, and lightgray. When you want a shade that is not on the list, use a hex color.\n- This applies to every color string in your code, including ones that only run when a condition is true, such as inside an if. Before you answer, check each color string against the list.\n- Keep everything on the 400 by 400 stage. Make sure text fits (about 10 pixels per character at height 20). Keep widths and heights from going negative. Convert numbers with str() before draw_text. Set a stroke color before draw_line. Keep notes between 48 and 84.\n- Make the picture work for every count from 3 to 10 and for the range in MY NUMBERS. Work out bar width and spacing from count. Never use a fixed width that overlaps at 10. Do not add checks for counts the plan rules out.\n- Use pause() inside the loop so the picture builds one number at a time. Do not add play(); the file already has one.\n\nTeaching\n- Use only commands and expressions students have seen so far. Do not use round(), one-line if/else expressions, or other shortcuts. Use int() to show a rounded number, and use a regular if statement for choices.\n- In your explanation, say in a sentence or two what each slot's code draws and which variables it uses. List every color name you used and confirm each is on the list. Tell the student what to predict before they run it."
      }
    },
    "start_sources": {
      "files": {
        "0": {
          "id": "0",
          "name": "story_plan.txt",
          "contents": "MY STORY\nWhat will you count? (one sentence, for example: \"the minutes I practice each day\")\n\n\n\nMY NUMBERS\nHow many numbers will you enter (3 to 10)?\nWhat do the numbers measure, and what is a normal range (for example, 10 to 60)?\n\n\n\nMY HAND TRACE\nMake up your first three numbers. After each one, write the total so far.\nNumber 1:      Total so far:\nNumber 2:      Total so far:\nNumber 3:      Total so far:\nIf you stopped after three numbers, what would the average be?\nDoes the total split evenly among the three numbers? (Is it divisible by 3?)\n\n\n\nMY PICTURE\nWhat should your picture look like? Finish these sentences.\nEach number will be a ______ (for example: bar, square, circle, dot).\nThe bigger the number, the shape will be ______ (for example: taller, larger, darker, higher).\nThe average will show up as ______ (for example: a line across the picture, one big shape, a label).\n\n\n\nMY AI PROMPT\nWrite what you will ask AI Tutor to draw. Use this pattern, then add your own details.\nDraw one ______ for each number. Use j for ______ and value for ______.\nThe biggest value is ______.\nAfter the loop, use average to draw ______.\nOnly draw. Do not calculate the total or the average.",
          "folderId": "0",
          "active": false
        },
        "1": {
          "id": "1",
          "name": "main.py",
          "contents": "from theater import *\n\n# ----- YOUR DATA STORY: change these to match your plan -----\nstory = \"My data story\"\ncount = 7\n\n# AI SLOT 1: ask AI Tutor for drawing code that sets up your picture\n# (background, title, labels). Paste it here. It should only draw.\n\n\n# YOUR CODE: start total at 0\n\n\nfor j in range(count):\n    value = 0    # YOUR CODE: replace 0 so value is the number from input(), turned into an int\n    # YOUR CODE: add value to total\n\n    # AI SLOT 2: ask AI Tutor for drawing code for this one number. It can use j and value.\n    # Paste it here, inside the loop.\n\n\n# YOUR CODE: set average to the total divided by count\n\n# YOUR CODE: set splits_evenly to True when total divides evenly by count (use %), and False if not\n\n# AI SLOT 3: ask AI Tutor for drawing code that shows the average. It can use average and splits_evenly.\n# Paste it here.\n\n\n# YOUR CODE: print the total, the average, and splits_evenly\n\nplay()",
          "folderId": "0",
          "active": false
        },
        "2": {
          "id": "2",
          "name": "story_review.txt",
          "contents": "MY TESTING\nRun your program with numbers you choose. Check the total, the average, and whether the total splits evenly by hand.\nNumbers I typed:\nTotal I worked out by hand:\nAverage I worked out by hand:\nDoes the total split evenly by hand? (True or False):\nTotal from my program:\nAverage from my program:\nSplits evenly from my program:\nDid they match? If not, what did you change?\n\n\n\nMY PARTNER CHECK\nYour partner reads your numbers (not your code) and guesses the average. Then run it.\nPartner's guess for the average:\nActual average:\nHow close was the guess?\nIn one sentence, what story does your picture tell?\n\n\n\nMY REFLECTION\nWhat did you ask AI Tutor to do?\nWhich parts of its code did you keep, change, or reject, and why?\nWhich lines did you write yourself?\nOne sentence: what does your data show?",
          "folderId": "0",
          "active": false
        }
      },
      "folders": {
      },
      "openFiles": [
        "0",
        "1",
        "2"
      ]
    },
